Command Reference — SIEM Detection Lab

A chronological log of every command used to build and test this project.
Replace <placeholder> values with your own if reproducing this lab 


1. macOS Agent — Install & Register

Download and install the Wazuh agent, registering it to the manager:

curl -so wazuh-agent.pkg https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/macos/wazuh-agent-4.14.6-1.arm64.pkg && echo "WAZUH_MANAGER='<your-manager-address>.cloud.wazuh.com' WAZUH_AGENT_NAME='<agent-name>' WAZUH_REGISTRATION_PASSWORD='<registration-password>'" > /tmp/wazuh_envs && sudo installer -pkg ./wazuh-agent.pkg -target /

Load the agent as a launch daemon:

sudo launchctl load /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.wazuh.agent.plist

2. macOS Detection Tests

Test 1: SSH brute-force attempt (required enabling Remote Login in System Settings > General > Sharing first):

ssh fakeuser@localhost
(entered wrong password 3-5 times)

Test 2: Sudo / privilege escalation event:

sudo touch /etc/testfile123
sudo rm /etc/testfile123
sudo whoami

Test 3: File Integrity Monitoring (FIM):

touch ~/Desktop/testfile_fim.txt
echo "test content" >> ~/Desktop/testfile_fim.txt
rm ~/Desktop/testfile_fim.txt

Investigating a rootcheck "hidden port" anomaly alert:

lsof -i :55555
sudo lsof -i :55555

3. AWS EC2 — Connect to the Linux Target

Navigate to the downloaded key pair and lock down permissions:

cd ~/Downloads
chmod 400 siem-lab-key.pem

SSH into the new Ubuntu instance:

ssh -i siem-lab-key.pem ubuntu@<ec2-public-ip>

Update the system:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y

4. Linux Agent — Install & Register

Download and install the Wazuh agent (.deb):

curl -so wazuh-agent.deb https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/apt/pool/main/w/wazuh-agent/wazuh-agent_4.14.6-1_amd64.deb && sudo WAZUH_MANAGER='<your-manager-address>.cloud.wazuh.com' WAZUH_AGENT_NAME='<agent-name>' dpkg -i ./wazuh-agent.deb

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable wazuh-agent
sudo systemctl start wazuh-agent
sudo systemctl status wazuh-agent

Check the agent log for registration errors:

sudo tail -50 /var/ossec/logs/ossec.log

Fixing a failed registration (missing password):

sudo /var/ossec/bin/agent-auth -m <your-manager-address>.cloud.wazuh.com -P '<registration-password>'
sudo systemctl restart wazuh-agent

Confirm it worked:

sudo tail -20 /var/ossec/logs/ossec.log

5. SSH Brute-Force Test — Troubleshooting & Execution

Initial attempt failed instantly (password auth disabled by default on AWS Ubuntu):

ssh fakeuser@<ec2-public-ip>
(result: Permission denied (publickey))

Reconnect to the instance to fix SSH config:

ssh -i siem-lab-key.pem ubuntu@<ec2-public-ip>

sudo sed -i 's/#PasswordAuthentication yes/PasswordAuthentication yes/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
sudo sed -i 's/PasswordAuthentication no/PasswordAuthentication yes/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
sudo systemctl restart ssh
sudo passwd ubuntu (set a test password for the ubuntu user)

Still failed — checked for a config override:

sudo grep -r "PasswordAuthentication" /etc/ssh/
(found: /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60-cloudimg-settings.conf:PasswordAuthentication no)

Fixed the override file specifically:

sudo sed -i 's/PasswordAuthentication no/PasswordAuthentication yes/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60-cloudimg-settings.conf
sudo systemctl restart ssh

Verified the fix:

sudo grep -r "PasswordAuthentication" /etc/ssh/
(all entries now show "yes")

exit

From the Mac — the actual brute-force simulation:

ssh fakeuser@<ec2-public-ip>
(entered wrong password 3+ times)
Result: Wazuh correctly escalated this to a high-severity alert (rule level 10, MITRE ATT&CK T1110 - Brute Force)
